```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  fig.path = "./figure/big_data/"
)
library(benchmarkme)
library(chunkhooks)
hook_benchmark()
set.seed(123)
```

This vignette will demonstrate how to work with large datasets in tidypopgen.

This includes whole-genome data. *Note* that tidypopgen only handles variant sites, so if you are using a whole-genome dataset make sure it has been previously subsetted to variant sites only. Think very carefully before merging this kind of data during your pipeline!

The latest version of tidypopgen has implemented a new option to minimise the memory footprint of very large datasets. As a large chunk of memory is occupied by loci names, the `gen_tibble()` function now offers the user the option to switch loci names from character strings to integers, reducing the memory used by the gen_tibble object.

# Getting the data

## Downloading the SGDP data

We will demonstrate the new capacity of tidypopgen using a subset of the [Simons Genome Diversity Project (SGDP)](https://reichdata.hms.harvard.edu/pub/datasets/sgdp/), containing 10 individuals each from Africa, Oceania, America and West Eurasia. The data are too large to download as part of this article, but the scripts in the [`data-raw/sgdp` folder](https://github.com/EvolEcolGroup/tidypopgen/tree/HEAD/data-raw/sgdp) of the tidypopgen repository let you create the dataset yourself if you wish to follow along.

The 40 individuals are listed in the files `afr_subset.txt`, `oceania_subset.txt`, `america_subset.txt` and `west_eur_subset.txt`. These were created by `s01_create_sgdp_subset_metadata.R`, which takes the first 10 fully public individuals from each region in the SGDP metadata.

SGDP provides a VCF for each individual, but distributes them as a single 57 GB archive containing all 279 public individuals. The shell script `s02_create_sgdp_subset.sh` downloads only the parts of the archive that hold our 40 individuals (about 8.7 GB), checks them against the checksums provided by SGDP, and merges them into a single VCF, `all_subset.vcf.gz`, with [bcftools](https://samtools.github.io/bcftools/). You will need `bcftools` and `curl` installed and available on your `PATH`. From the root of a copy of the tidypopgen repository, run:

```{bash, eval = FALSE}
bash data-raw/sgdp/s02_create_sgdp_subset.sh vignettes/articles/data/sgdp
```

This writes `all_subset.vcf.gz` and its index to `vignettes/articles/data/sgdp/`, which is where we read it from below (the paths in this article are relative to `vignettes/articles/`). The individual VCFs are kept in `vignettes/articles/data/sgdp/sgdp_vcfs/`, so that an interrupted download can be resumed by running the script again; once the merged VCF has been created, they can be deleted.

Note that each SGDP VCF only contains the sites at which that individual carries a non-reference allele. When merging, a site that is absent from an individual's VCF is therefore set to homozygous for the reference allele (with the `--missing-to-ref` option of `bcftools merge`). Without this option, every homozygous reference genotype would be recorded as missing. The cost is that a site that was not sequenced in an individual is also set to homozygous reference, which is a reasonable approximation for these high-coverage genomes.

## Reading a large VCF into a gen_tibble

Now that we have our data we can begin by loading the necessary libraries:

```{r}
library(tidypopgen)
library(ggplot2)
```

In many descriptive population genetic analyses, where the end goal is to visualise populations (e.g., PCA) or calculate a summary statistic (e.g. F~ST~), the names of your loci are not needed for the analysis. In these cases, we can save a lot of memory (over half of the memory footprint of the `gen_tibble`) by switching the loci names from strings to integers.

This option is only enabled when loading from a .vcf file. To do this, we need to set parameter `names_as_int` to TRUE when creating the gen_tibble:

```{r read_gen_tibble, eval = FALSE}
sgdp <-
  gen_tibble("data/sgdp/all_subset.vcf.gz",
    quiet = TRUE,
    backingfile = "data/sgdp/all_subset",
    names_as_int = TRUE
  )
```

```{r gt_load, results="hide", echo=FALSE, eval=TRUE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
if (file.exists("data/sgdp/all_subset.gt")) {
  sgdp <-
    gt_load("data/sgdp/all_subset.gt",
      reattach_to = "data/sgdp/all_subset.rds"
    )
} else {
  sgdp <-
    gen_tibble("data/sgdp/all_subset.vcf.gz",
      backingfile = "data/sgdp/all_subset",
      names_as_int = TRUE
    )
}

metadata <-
  read.table("../../data-raw/sgdp/SGDP_metadata.279public.21signedLetter.44Fan.samples.csv",
    header = TRUE,
    sep = ";"
  )
metadata <- metadata %>%
  rename(id = Illumina_ID) %>%
  rename(population = Population_ID)
sgdp <- left_join(sgdp, metadata, by = "id")
sgdp$Region[sgdp$Region == "WestEurasia"] <- "West Eurasia"
```

When we now call our loci tibble with `show_loci()`, we can see that the loci names are now integers:

```{r show_loci}
show_loci(sgdp)
```

The rsID's of each SNP have been removed, but all other metadata (chromosome, position, genetic distance, and alleles) are retained. We can check the memory footprint of the gen_tibble using:

```{r check_size}
format(object.size(sgdp), units = "auto", digits = 2)
```

## Setting up cores

Because tidypopgen utilizes disk storage through file-backed matrices (FBM), we can run the following pipeline on a modern laptop without running out of RAM. Here, we use a machine with a `r benchmarkme::get_cpu()` CPU and `r as.character(print(benchmarkme::get_ram(), unit_system = "iec"))` of RAM.

Before we begin, we will limit the number of cores used by the parallelised BLAS library with `bigparallelr::set_blas_ncores()`, and by the package data.table with `data.table::setDTthreads()`. *Note* You may need to adjust `n_cores` according to your own hardware.

```{r}
n_cores <- 20
data.table::setDTthreads(n_cores)
bigparallelr::set_blas_ncores(n_cores)
```

# Quality control for large datasets

## Individual and loci reports

All the normal functionality of tidypopgen is preserved, as our `qc_report` functions exemplify:

```{r indiv_report, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp_indiv_rep <- qc_report_indiv(sgdp)
```

```{r loci_report, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp <- sgdp %>% group_by(Region)
sgdp_loci_rep <- qc_report_loci(sgdp)
sgdp <- sgdp %>% ungroup()
```

However, it is important to be mindful that analyses on very large datasets will produce large outputs:

```{r}
format(object.size(sgdp_loci_rep), units = "auto", digits = 2)
```

In this case, a loci report on `r count_loci(sgdp)` loci for `r nrow(sgdp)` individuals occupies approximately `r format(object.size(sgdp_loci_rep), units = "auto", digits = 2)` of memory.

Let's plot the overview of the loci report:

```{r, autoplot_loci}
autoplot(sgdp_loci_rep,
  maf_threshold = 0.05,
  miss_threshold = 0.05
)
```

We can see from the Upset plot above that MAF filtering will remove a large number of loci. WGS data require heavy filtering for MAF to avoid including uninformative loci in analyses (e.g. sequencing errors or private mutations).

On the other hand, no loci fail the missingness threshold. Because sites absent from an individual's VCF were set to homozygous reference when we merged the VCFs, there are no missing genotypes in this dataset. We can check the number of loci with any missing genotypes:

```{r}
sum(sgdp_loci_rep$missingness > 0)
```

When objects are no longer needed, or once results outputs have been saved, we can remove them using `rm()` to keep memory usage under control:

```{r}
rm(sgdp_loci_rep)
```

## Filtering

We can then proceed with basic data filtering in the same way as working with any other gen_tibble object.

We filter for minor allele frequency, choosing to select loci with MAF greater than 5%:

```{r maf_filter, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp <- sgdp %>%
  select_loci_if(loci_maf(genotypes) > 0.05)
cat("Loci remaining:", count_loci(sgdp))
```

With other datasets, we would also remove loci with too many missing genotypes (e.g. with `select_loci_if(loci_missingness(genotypes) < 0.1)`), and then impute the remaining missing genotypes with `gt_impute_simple()`, as LD clumping and PCA do not accept missing values. Our dataset has no missing genotypes, so we can skip both steps.

## Updating the backing file

After removing loci, we update the file backing matrix with `gt_update_backingfile()`, so that it only contains the loci that we kept:

```{r update_backingfile, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp <- gt_update_backingfile(sgdp, quiet = TRUE,
                              backingfile = tempfile())
```

This will help us with the next step (LD clumping).

## LD clumping

Then we can LD clump the dataset to remove loci in linkage disequilibrium.

With many loci, this step can be a little more time consuming, so it is important to remember to specify the `n_cores` parameter to speed things up:

```{r ld_clump, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp <- sgdp %>%
  select_loci_if(loci_ld_clump(genotypes,
    n_cores = n_cores,
    thr_r2 = 0.2
  ))
cat("Loci remaining:", count_loci(sgdp))
```

# Analysis

## PCA

Now that we have a clean dataset, we can proceed with our analysis by plotting a PCA:

```{r pca, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp_pca <- sgdp %>% gt_pca_partialSVD()
```

```{r PCA}
autoplot(sgdp_pca, type = "scores") +
  aes(colour = sgdp$Region) +
  labs(color = "Region")
```

Our PCA separates the four regions. PC1 (5.98% of the variance) runs from the African individuals on the left, through West Eurasia, to the American individuals on the right, and PC2 (4.48%) separates the Oceanian individuals at the top from the American individuals at the bottom. The individuals from America and West Eurasia form tight clusters, whereas the African individuals are spread out along PC1: the Mbuti, Ju/'hoan North, Yoruba, Mandenka and Dinka individuals are on the far left, followed by the Masai and Somali individuals from East Africa, while the two Mozabite individuals from Algeria lie close to the West Eurasian cluster. This is consistent with North and East African populations carrying some West Eurasian-related ancestry. The Oceanian individuals form two groups: the Papuan, Bougainville and Australian individuals at the top, and the Dusun (from Borneo), Hawaiian and Maori individuals closer to the centre of the plot.

## DAPC

We can also perform a discriminant analysis of principal components (DAPC) using `gt_dapc`.

```{r dapc, benchmark = TRUE}
sgdp_dapc <- gt_dapc(sgdp_pca,
  pop = as.factor(sgdp$Region)
)
```

```{r DAPC_components_plot}
autoplot(sgdp_dapc, type = "components")
```

Our DAPC autoplot shows that most individuals are assigned to their own region's group with a probability close to one, showing that there is genetic differentiation between the continental regions. The exceptions are consistent with the PCA above: the two Mozabite individuals are assigned to the West Eurasian group (with probabilities of 0.94 and 0.99), the Somali individual has a small probability (about 0.01) of West Eurasian membership, and the Maori individual has a probability of 0.16 of West Eurasian membership. These individuals may have admixed ancestry.

# Pairwise F~ST~

Finally, to examine the differentiation between regions, we can calculate pairwise F~ST~.

```{r pairwise_fst, benchmark = TRUE}
grouped_sgdp <- sgdp %>% group_by(Region)
pairwise_fst <- grouped_sgdp %>% pairwise_pop_fst(
  n_cores = n_cores,
  type = "pairwise"
)

autoplot(pairwise_fst,
  order = function(x) {
    stats::hclust(stats::as.dist(x), method = "complete")
  }
) +
  scale_fill_viridis_c() +
  theme(
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = -60, hjust = 0, size = 6),
    axis.text.y = element_text(angle = 0, hjust = 1, size = 6)
  ) +
  labs(
    x = element_blank(),
    y = element_blank(),
    fill = expression("Pairwise F"[ST])
  )
```

The pairwise F~ST~ values are all small (between 0.03 and 0.07), as is typical of human populations. America is the most differentiated region, and Africa and America are the most differentiated pair (F~ST~ = 0.069). Africa and West Eurasia are the least differentiated pair (0.033), closely followed by Oceania and West Eurasia (0.034); the low value between Africa and West Eurasia partly reflects the West Eurasian-related ancestry of the Mozabite, Somali and Masai individuals in our African sample. Note that with only 10 individuals per region, these estimates are imprecise.

# Performance summary

Here is a summary of the time taken (in seconds) to perform each step using a large dataset:

```{r echo=FALSE}
chunk_order <- knitr::all_labels() # Get order
# Chunks that have benchmarks
benchmarked_chunks <- names(chunkhooks::benchmarks)

ordered_benchmark_chunks <- chunk_order[chunk_order %in% benchmarked_chunks]

benchmark_results <- data.frame(
  step = ordered_benchmark_chunks,
  time = NA_real_
)
for (i in seq_along(benchmark_results$step)) {
  this_step <- benchmark_results$step[i]
  benchmark_results$time[i] <- round(chunkhooks::benchmarks[[this_step]], 2)
}
total <- c("Total", sum(benchmark_results$time))
benchmark_results <- rbind(benchmark_results, total)
benchmark_results
```

# Key takeaways

- **Large data don't need a large machine.** `gen_tibble` objects are file-backed, so the genotypes stay on disk and only what you are working on is held in memory.
- **Use `names_as_int = TRUE` when reading a VCF** to reduce the memory taken by locus names. The trade-off is that the original locus IDs are not kept (chromosome and position are).
- **Set your cores.** Limit BLAS and `data.table` threads at the start, and pass `n_cores` to the functions that support it (e.g. LD clumping and F~ST~).
- **Large analyses make large outputs.** Reports such as `qc_report_loci()` can be big, so save what you need and `rm()` the rest.
